WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.149
Hello.

2
00:00:10.759 --> 00:00:15.559
I will speak in Bulgarian, and I will try to fit into the short

3
00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:18.480
time that each of the panel participants has.

4
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:24.480
The question is: what is the future of mobility?

5
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:31.240
For us, the future of mobility is a question that is rather

6
00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:34.910
phrased like this: from ownership to access.

7
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.200
Do we need to own every vehicle

8
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.109
that we want to use, or is it better to use

9
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.949
any such vehicle that is available as some kind of service?

10
00:00:48.039 --> 00:00:51.640
Here I would like to tell you a short little story.

11
00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:56.520
Imagine you leave home for work in the morning,

12
00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.390
and you head to the office where you work.

13
00:01:01.399 --> 00:01:04.720
You are done, that is, you have reached your destination,

14
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.119
but then the search for a parking space begins.

15
00:01:07.119 --> 00:01:09.439
First street, second street, third street.

16
00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:11.040
Yes, there is a space, but it is far away.

17
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.640
It is not convenient. You have arrived, but the trip has not

18
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.510
ended.

19
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:25.600
So do we really not need to

20
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.029
own a personal car?

21
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.669
Here I touch on several important things

22
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.989
related to the personal car.

23
00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:37.360
I assume each of you owns a car.

24
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.070
Some own more than one.

25
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.629
Here the situation is as follows.

26
00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:43.949
For most people,

27
00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:48.320
a car is a sum of money, never mind how much.

28
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.510
Make up a number yourselves, for example 5,000.

29
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.880
People in general see the situation in the following way.

30
00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.240
A car: we buy it and forget about it, right.

31
00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.790
It gives us freedom, we travel and so on.

32
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.750
The thing is that a car does not end with the purchase price alone.

33
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.429
With the keys we get quite a lot of

34
00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:13.280
additional commitments and costs that come with it,

35
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.760
which most people perhaps do not pay attention to,

36
00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:20.560
right: we have the purchase price, yes, we have fuel or electricity,

37
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.840
depending on what each of us chose to drive, but then come the other

38
00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.680
things that every car needs to have paid for.

39
00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.789
These are liability insurance, comprehensive insurance if you choose,

40
00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.360
tax, inspection. This is on an annual basis.

41
00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.640
We have maintenance, which is also a key item,

42
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.760
everyone knows it: servicing, oil, filters, brake discs and pads, and so

43
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.509
on. This happens regularly.

44
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.350
This is also a cost. And on top of that we have

45
00:02:50.599 --> 00:02:54.519
repairs, especially those that happen at the most inconvenient

46
00:02:54.519 --> 00:02:58.120
moment before a trip. We want to leave, something has broken,

47
00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.830
the trip is ruined and so on.

48
00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:07.310
Another key point here is that we own a car,

49
00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.400
and according to statistics, over 90% of the time it is not used.

50
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.270
It stands parked. I assume that wherever you all live,

51
00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.709
when you step out in front of your home, you see the parking spaces.

52
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.080
There are always some cars that just sit there.

53
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.149
They are like monuments.

54
00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.519
50% of the parking spaces are full of such cars.

55
00:03:28.519 --> 00:03:32.360
Nothing moves, or people simply move them from one spot to

56
00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.760
another to keep their place in front of the building, and they are used

57
00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:42.599
very rarely indeed. This is also tied to

58
00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:46.760
the age of cars, perhaps in Bulgaria,

59
00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.280
which is quite high and serious.

60
00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.920
The average age of a car in Bulgaria is 21 years, compared to

61
00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:57.040
the European Union, where the figure is almost half, and there it is

62
00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:03.629
12.7 years. An older car, more harmful emissions.

63
00:04:04.239 --> 00:04:08.120
Older cars do not meet the necessary environmental requirements

64
00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:13.920
and have no systems to control harmful emissions,

65
00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.789
which in turn leads to worse air quality.

66
00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:24.520
This year we attended an event in Sofia, and there the GATE Institute

67
00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:29.240
showed exactly digital twins and the monitoring of the air,

68
00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.870
the weather conditions in Sofia, on which

69
00:04:32.919 --> 00:04:36.270
the old car fleet certainly has some influence.

70
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.950
And last but not least, we have a problem with parking,

71
00:04:41.479 --> 00:04:43.029
parking lots, a lack of parking spaces

72
00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.039
and the time lost in traffic,

73
00:04:47.039 --> 00:04:50.840
where, as you can see, these hours are per year, and this is

74
00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:54.120
an average for the country; for Sofia the number is a bit higher.

75
00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.790
For Varna it is almost the same, about 93 to 95

76
00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:00.630
hours per year.

77
00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.550
Do we really need a car for every trip?

78
00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:10.160
Here comes the question of whether we can work out, from the route and

79
00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.080
where we want to get from point A to point B,

80
00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.630
whether we always need a car.

81
00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:21.469
Here everyone can take

82
00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:25.869
a look back at the previous week: what your trips were.

83
00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:30.469
And I can say it is a very good option.

84
00:05:31.639 --> 00:05:35.800
Say the public transport stop is not close to you,

85
00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.800
and so on, you can always leave

86
00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.639
home, take a bicycle, combine it with city

87
00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:46.600
transport, or if your office is close, it can be entirely

88
00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.430
with a shared bicycle.

89
00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.000
Of course, a rental car.

90
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.440
Maybe for a weekend trip out of town.

91
00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:00.189
And if you use it daily and really need a car

92
00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:04.600
all the time, here comes the monthly car subscription,

93
00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:09.000
where the cost is predictable and you can forecast costs,

94
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.240
right, on an annual basis. How much would I spend on a car

95
00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:14.430
if I really need it all the time.

96
00:06:16.039 --> 00:06:17.749
But what do we gain if

97
00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:20.599
we are not tied to a car like that?

98
00:06:20.599 --> 00:06:24.440
This applies to individuals and to companies

99
00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:28.319
absolutely equally. We gain more choice when we have alternatives,

100
00:06:28.319 --> 00:06:30.270
when we have a good service that works.

101
00:06:31.039 --> 00:06:34.880
We have control over costs: we choose a rental car,

102
00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.400
we know, we want it for two days, our period is two days,

103
00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.029
we want these extras.

104
00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:43.520
The price is clear; with a subscription it is even more predictable,

105
00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:46.070
because the prices there are even better.

106
00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:48.630
Fewer commitments

107
00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:54.080
and no commitments regarding maintenance and all such things

108
00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.510
that I listed on the previous slides.

109
00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:03.910
And here comes my own part, in the company.

110
00:07:04.479 --> 00:07:09.639
This is the moment to mention that Toprent is making a

111
00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:11.790
smooth transition to TopMobility.

112
00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:15.840
We are rebranding, and very soon you will learn a lot more on this,

113
00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:20.280
but from a car rental company we aim to make the transition

114
00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:23.790
to a company that provides mobility, any type of mobility.

115
00:07:24.879 --> 00:07:28.510
Directly, I deal with the shared bicycles in the company,

116
00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:31.630
where the idea of Smart Cities comes in.

117
00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:34.429
Here we use geofencing technology

118
00:07:35.599 --> 00:07:38.560
to control the parking of the bicycles.

119
00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:40.189
We monitor on a daily basis

120
00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:45.560
the usage of the bicycles,

121
00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.400
the demand, their movement around the city.

122
00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:52.240
When needed, we relocate them to the zones where the need is greatest.

123
00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:58.120
Of course, this happens with a team,

124
00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:01.720
an operations team, my team, which maintains this every day

125
00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:05.469
so that it can exist. We do maintenance, we repair the bicycles,

126
00:08:06.319 --> 00:08:10.280
we replace batteries, we relocate the bicycles to the most needed spots in

127
00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:14.550
the city. Now picture this with 500 bicycles,

128
00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:19.960
five locations in the country, and the numbers speak for themselves.

129
00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:24.560
We serve over 55,000 users, and I dare say

130
00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:27.070
that this number is quite interesting.

131
00:08:27.639 --> 00:08:31.840
In the period we have operated, we have saved almost 100 tonnes of harmful

132
00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:35.149
emissions by using our shared bicycles,

133
00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.399
and I realise very quickly that my time is running out,

134
00:08:39.399 --> 00:08:44.080
I will give you just one example from Byala municipality, where we ran

135
00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:46.360
a pilot with them last year.

136
00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:49.389
They approached us, they needed it and wanted to provide an alternative

137
00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:53.240
mobility service for their tourists and visitors to the town,

138
00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:56.949
and over a period of three and a half months we deployed 20 bicycles,

139
00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:01.640
set up five parking zones, and customers made over

140
00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:05.509
1,500 rides and generated more than 3,000 km.

141
00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:11.040
Also, last but not least, we have saved emissions, and perhaps here

142
00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:15.320
at the bottom, but not least, the customers were extremely

143
00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:19.270
satisfied. They rated us an excellent 4.8 out of five.

144
00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:25.000
And finally, I put this question to you.

145
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:26.389
Do we really have to own

146
00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:31.640
every vehicle we need, or can we trust

147
00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:37.800
services like ours, which benefit all of you when you need

148
00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:38.700
to get around.
